  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 416 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low stretching from the Black\nHills into western Nebraska/northeast Wyoming, with high over\nthe Ohio River Valley. Upper level analysis indicates split \nlows with the first one over northern Nevada, and the second \ndropping along the lee of the Canadian Rockies through Alberta. \nOver our area, southwest flow aloft is in place, while at the \nsurface light east/southeast flow continues. Some patches of fog\nhave developed over the southwest.\n\nFor today, surface low stretches towards the northeast,\nelongating into the Red River Valley by this evening. In the\nmeantime, southern upper low opens up as the Alberta low becomes\nthe primary system with trough dropping to the south-southwest.\nRain chances develop today over northern locations, then\nthroughout most of the area as the upper low drops into southern\nSaskatchewan. Chances switch towards light snow over western and\nnorthern locations through the night as cooler air wraps in\nbehind the low, but any accumulations are expected to be on the\nlighter side. \n\nOn Saturday, some light snow may linger into the morning around\nthe Turtle Mountains area. Otherwise, cooler air continues to\nwrap into the area with highs in the 30s over most locations,\nmaybe some lower to mid 40s over our far southeast. Some low\nsnow chances return Saturday night, lingering into Sunday, over\nthe north as the trough slides through and off to the east.\n\nA mainly dry pattern then develops for the upcoming week, though\na variety of modest waves pass through which could bring an\noccasional low chance for snow, most notably late Monday into\nTuesday. Temperatures remain cool through the start of the \nweek, with a modest rebound possible mid-week.",
